WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.680
Usually, you know, when we talk about a medical diagnosis, there's this expectation of precision.

2
00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:05.680
It feels like engineering.

3
00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:06.680
Right.

4
00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:07.680
It's very black and white.

5
00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:08.680
Yeah, exactly.

6
00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:13.280
Like, you break your arm, the x-ray shows that dragged white line in the bone, and the doctor

7
00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:15.400
just points at the screen.

8
00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:19.240
Broken or not broken, it's clean, and honestly, it's comforting, you know?

9
00:00:19.240 --> 00:00:20.240
Oh, totally.

10
00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:23.360
We love things to be visible and easily categorized.

11
00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:24.360
We do.

12
00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:31.440
But then you step into the world of neurodevelopment, trauma, and acute mental health crises, and

13
00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:34.800
suddenly that x-ray machine is completely useless.

14
00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:37.960
The diagnostic landscape is just entirely murky.

15
00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:41.320
It really is the absolute definition of diagnostic muddy waters.

16
00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:42.320
Yeah.

17
00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:45.480
I mean, you can't just draw blood to measure someone's level of despair.

18
00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:46.480
Right.

19
00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:49.720
And you definitely can't run a scan to see if they're going to harm themselves or someone

20
00:00:49.720 --> 00:00:51.040
else in the next 24 hours.

21
00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:56.040
You have to rely on behavioral cues, human psychology, and incredibly structured assessment

22
00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:57.040
tools.

23
00:00:57.040 --> 00:00:59.640
And that is exactly where we are heading today.

24
00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:04.560
Welcome to this deep dive into the high stakes, high pressure world of crisis management.

25
00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:07.060
It's a heavy topic, but so important.

26
00:01:07.060 --> 00:01:08.080
It really is.

27
00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:14.040
And our source material today is this rigorous set of notes from a licensing exam prep guide,

28
00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:16.480
specifically, Pass Ready Prep by Keek.

29
00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:18.840
We're looking at their crisis and safety module.

30
00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:19.840
Yeah.

31
00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:24.960
This is the exact material clinical professional study to handle worst case scenarios.

32
00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:25.960
Exactly.

33
00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:29.360
But our mission for this deep dive, for you listening right now, isn't just to help you

34
00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:31.100
pass a therapy exam.

35
00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:35.640
We want to extract the universal framework that professionals use to make literal life

36
00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:37.800
or death decisions without panicking.

37
00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:39.720
Which is incredibly hard to do.

38
00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:40.720
Right.

39
00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:44.120
And the goal is to show you how these underlying principles can actually apply to staying

40
00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:48.280
cool and methodical under any extreme pressure in your own life.

41
00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:52.920
What's fascinating here is that in this specific clinical domain, a wrong answer doesn't just

42
00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:54.760
mean failing a test.

43
00:01:54.760 --> 00:01:56.760
It means someone in real life gets hurt.

44
00:01:56.760 --> 00:01:57.760
Yeah.

45
00:01:57.760 --> 00:01:58.760
The stakes are ultimate.

46
00:01:58.760 --> 00:01:59.760
Exactly.

47
00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:04.440
The exam in real world clinical practice really is designed to test one fundamental thing.

48
00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:05.440
Yeah.

49
00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:09.600
Can you stay calm and think in the correct automatic order when everything around you

50
00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:10.600
is chaotic?

51
00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:13.600
Because our biology is fighting us the whole time, right?

52
00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:15.080
Oh, 100%.

53
00:02:15.080 --> 00:02:18.520
Some biology wants us to panic when we see someone in distress.

54
00:02:18.520 --> 00:02:24.800
Our brains flood with cortisol, the logic centers shut down, and we just react impulsively.

55
00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:29.760
So these notes are essentially a codified system for overriding that biological panic.

56
00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:30.760
Precisely.

57
00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:31.760
Okay.

58
00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:34.800
Let's unpack this because that idea of an automatic order is really the secret sauce

59
00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:36.520
to surviving chaos.

60
00:02:36.520 --> 00:02:39.960
The source material outlines four unbreakable pillars.

61
00:02:39.960 --> 00:02:40.960
Yeah.

62
00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:44.160
And these four rules basically carry the entire crisis category.

63
00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:48.400
If you understand these, you understand the architecture of professional crisis response.

64
00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:49.440
So what's rule number one?

65
00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:52.640
Rule number one is you assess before you act, always.

66
00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:55.920
Rule number two is you choose the least restrictive option that still keeps the person

67
00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:56.920
safe.

68
00:02:56.920 --> 00:02:58.000
List restrictive, all right.

69
00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:03.080
Then rule number three, which kind of runs quietly underneath everything, is you document

70
00:03:03.080 --> 00:03:04.160
every single step.

71
00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:08.800
And rule number four is when you are unsure, you consult.

72
00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:12.200
Assess least restrictive option, document, and consult.

73
00:03:12.200 --> 00:03:14.400
That reminds me so much of emergency room triage.

74
00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:16.080
Oh, that's a great comparison.

75
00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:17.080
Right.

76
00:03:17.080 --> 00:03:21.400
Like when someone bursts through the doors of an ER with a major trauma, doctors don't

77
00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:24.460
just start performing surgery the second they see blood.

78
00:03:24.460 --> 00:03:28.120
They have an automatic, unshakable order of operations.

79
00:03:28.120 --> 00:03:30.720
Airway, breathing, circulation.

80
00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:31.720
Exactly.

81
00:03:31.720 --> 00:03:36.720
They follow the protocol so that human panic doesn't override logic.

82
00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:39.960
The phrase the source material uses is perfect.

83
00:03:39.960 --> 00:03:41.000
Automatic beats clever.

84
00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:42.800
I love that phrase.

85
00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:46.680
Because when you are in a crisis, you don't want to try to be a creative genius.

86
00:03:46.680 --> 00:03:47.680
You want a system.

87
00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:48.920
Yeah, you really do.

88
00:03:48.920 --> 00:03:52.580
Because like we said, when you're flooded with adrenaline, your prefrontal cortex,

89
00:03:52.580 --> 00:03:55.400
the logical planning part of your brain just goes offline.

90
00:03:55.400 --> 00:03:57.040
You have to revert to your training.

91
00:03:57.040 --> 00:03:59.560
Which is why this applies to everyone, not just therapists.

92
00:03:59.560 --> 00:04:00.560
Simply.

93
00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:04.080
I want to point out to you listening that these four pillars are a masterclass in objective

94
00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:06.760
decision making during any crisis.

95
00:04:06.760 --> 00:04:10.120
Like a PR disaster or a financial crisis at work.

96
00:04:10.120 --> 00:04:11.120
Yes.

97
00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:13.040
Think about a massive financial emergency.

98
00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:18.600
Our instinct is to immediately fire someone, right, acting before assessing, or we implement

99
00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:23.240
these draconian new policies that paralyze the company, which violates the least restrictive

100
00:04:23.240 --> 00:04:24.240
option rule.

101
00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:25.240
Wow.

102
00:04:25.240 --> 00:04:26.240
Yeah.

103
00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:27.240
That happens all the time.

104
00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:30.840
Taking a breath to assess the actual damage, finding the solution that causes the least

105
00:04:30.840 --> 00:04:36.800
disruption while maintaining safety, documenting your process and bringing in an outside perspective,

106
00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:38.960
that framework is completely universal.

107
00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:43.540
So let's look at how those four pillars apply to the most immediate, severe crisis covered

108
00:04:43.540 --> 00:04:46.680
in our notes, which is acute suicide risk.

109
00:04:46.680 --> 00:04:50.040
We are talking about a scenario where the stakes literally cannot be higher.

110
00:04:50.040 --> 00:04:51.040
Right.

111
00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:55.240
And this is where that first rule assessed before you act becomes a strict non-negotiable

112
00:04:55.240 --> 00:04:56.240
sequence.

113
00:04:56.240 --> 00:04:59.960
You don't just ask a broad question like, are you okay?

114
00:04:59.960 --> 00:05:01.240
And jump to conclusions.

115
00:05:01.240 --> 00:05:03.120
You assess in a very specific order.

116
00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:04.120
Exactly.

117
00:05:04.120 --> 00:05:05.120
First is ideation.

118
00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:06.780
Is the thought of suicide actually there?

119
00:05:06.780 --> 00:05:08.280
If it is, you move to plan.

120
00:05:08.280 --> 00:05:10.040
Is there a specific plan in place?

121
00:05:10.040 --> 00:05:13.360
Then intent, do they actually mean to act on this plan?

122
00:05:13.360 --> 00:05:16.080
Ideation, plan, intent, what's next?

123
00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:17.080
Next is access.

124
00:05:17.080 --> 00:05:20.120
Do they have the means to carry out the plan?

125
00:05:20.120 --> 00:05:22.880
And finally, protective factors.

126
00:05:22.880 --> 00:05:24.880
What is pulling them toward living?

127
00:05:24.880 --> 00:05:30.360
So it's ideation, plan, intent, access, protective factors.

128
00:05:30.360 --> 00:05:34.920
Walk us through why that specific staircase of assessment is so critical.

129
00:05:34.920 --> 00:05:39.680
Why can't a professional just jump straight to asking about access to weapons?

130
00:05:39.680 --> 00:05:44.040
Because the level of restriction you apply to a person's life depends entirely on where

131
00:05:44.040 --> 00:05:45.040
they fall on those stairs.

132
00:05:45.040 --> 00:05:46.040
Okay.

133
00:05:46.040 --> 00:05:47.040
That makes sense.

134
00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:48.040
Right.

135
00:05:48.040 --> 00:05:50.160
So if there is no ideation, you don't interrogate them about a plan.

136
00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:53.700
But if there is a plan, you must assess access immediately.

137
00:05:53.700 --> 00:05:55.320
Because the risk level changes completely.

138
00:05:55.320 --> 00:05:56.320
Exactly.

139
00:05:56.320 --> 00:06:00.160
A client who has a detailed plan to use a firearm requires a totally different, highly

140
00:06:00.160 --> 00:06:04.400
restrictive emergency response if they actually own one versus if they don't.

141
00:06:04.400 --> 00:06:06.600
And professionals use a specific tool for this, right?

142
00:06:06.600 --> 00:06:07.600
Yeah.

143
00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:12.000
It's mentioned in the source, the Columbia Suicide Severity Rating Scale, or CSSRS.

144
00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:15.520
Based on where a person lands on that scale, the interventions stack up accordingly.

145
00:06:15.520 --> 00:06:19.360
Now, the notes point out a trap here regarding how professionals intervene.

146
00:06:19.360 --> 00:06:22.520
And I'm going to push back on this a bit because it seems totally counterintuitive.

147
00:06:22.520 --> 00:06:24.240
Oh, the no suicide contract.

148
00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:25.240
Yes.

149
00:06:25.240 --> 00:06:29.720
The material says, having a client sign a no suicide contract is a trap and an incorrect

150
00:06:29.720 --> 00:06:31.040
intervention.

151
00:06:31.040 --> 00:06:36.480
But in the corporate or legal world, signing a contract creates heavy psychological accountability.

152
00:06:36.480 --> 00:06:37.480
Sure.

153
00:06:37.480 --> 00:06:38.680
It feels very official.

154
00:06:38.680 --> 00:06:39.680
Right.

155
00:06:39.680 --> 00:06:44.640
So getting someone to physically sign a promise to stay safe, doesn't that create a vital

156
00:06:44.640 --> 00:06:47.680
psychological hurdle that stops them from acting?

157
00:06:47.680 --> 00:06:49.400
Why wouldn't we want that?

158
00:06:49.400 --> 00:06:54.040
If we connect this to the bigger picture, the flaw in that corporate logic becomes

159
00:06:54.040 --> 00:06:55.960
really obvious.

160
00:06:55.960 --> 00:06:57.600
Agony doesn't obey contracts.

161
00:06:57.600 --> 00:06:58.980
Oh, wow.

162
00:06:58.980 --> 00:07:00.240
Agony doesn't obey contracts.

163
00:07:00.240 --> 00:07:01.240
Yeah.

164
00:07:01.240 --> 00:07:04.880
No suicide contracts are not evidence-based because they're essentially asking someone

165
00:07:04.880 --> 00:07:11.120
in immense unbearable psychological pain to just promise not to feel that way.

166
00:07:11.120 --> 00:07:14.680
Or to suppress the only escape route their brain can currently see.

167
00:07:14.680 --> 00:07:15.680
Precisely.

168
00:07:15.680 --> 00:07:20.000
A contract might make the professional feel legally protected or give the illusion of

169
00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:26.680
control, but it does not equip the client with any actual tools to survive a dark night.

170
00:07:26.680 --> 00:07:30.720
It's basically demanding a result without providing a method.

171
00:07:30.720 --> 00:07:31.720
Exactly.

172
00:07:31.720 --> 00:07:36.400
Instead, the correct evidence-based intervention is collaborative safety planning.

173
00:07:36.400 --> 00:07:38.280
How is that different from a contract?

174
00:07:38.280 --> 00:07:42.820
A safety plan is a written document, sure, but the client helps build it.

175
00:07:42.820 --> 00:07:47.880
It outlines their personal internal warning signs, specific coping strategies they agree

176
00:07:47.880 --> 00:07:52.100
they can use, and exactly who they can call when things get dark.

177
00:07:52.100 --> 00:07:55.080
So it's an actionable toolkit, not a legalistic promise.

178
00:07:55.080 --> 00:07:56.080
Right.

179
00:07:56.080 --> 00:08:00.400
It's paired with means-restriction counseling, which focuses on the practical side working

180
00:08:00.400 --> 00:08:05.400
with the client or their family to temporarily remove access to lethal means, like locking

181
00:08:05.400 --> 00:08:07.000
up medications or firearms.

182
00:08:07.000 --> 00:08:10.480
So if you're looking at a list of options for how to help someone in crisis, the contract

183
00:08:10.480 --> 00:08:12.560
for safety is a total mirage.

184
00:08:12.560 --> 00:08:15.600
The other trap the notes highlight is the overreaction trap.

185
00:08:15.600 --> 00:08:19.800
And this directly violates our second pillar, choosing the least restrictive option.

186
00:08:19.800 --> 00:08:25.320
The human psychology trap here is immediately hospitalizing someone who merely expresses

187
00:08:25.320 --> 00:08:29.480
a passing thought of suicide, but who has no plan and no intent.

188
00:08:29.480 --> 00:08:31.840
Which feels like it would be a common reflex.

189
00:08:31.840 --> 00:08:34.280
It is, because people panic.

190
00:08:34.280 --> 00:08:39.520
But taking away someone's freedom by hospitalizing them against their will is the most restrictive

191
00:08:39.520 --> 00:08:41.800
option possible in mental health.

192
00:08:41.800 --> 00:08:46.080
So if you jump to that extreme without walking those assessment stairs, you're reacting

193
00:08:46.080 --> 00:08:47.440
out of your own fear.

194
00:08:47.440 --> 00:08:51.140
You're reacting out of anxiety and fear of liability, not clinical protocol.

195
00:08:51.140 --> 00:08:53.900
We see this overreaction trap everywhere, right?

196
00:08:53.900 --> 00:08:55.700
Back to that corporate analogy.

197
00:08:55.700 --> 00:09:00.220
An employee makes a minor passing error and a panicked manager immediately fires them.

198
00:09:00.220 --> 00:09:01.900
The most restrictive option.

199
00:09:01.900 --> 00:09:02.900
Exactly.

200
00:09:02.900 --> 00:09:06.520
Instead of putting them on a performance plan or just having a conversation, the manager

201
00:09:06.520 --> 00:09:10.660
is reacting to their own anxiety about the project failing, completely bypassing the

202
00:09:10.660 --> 00:09:11.660
assessment phase.

203
00:09:11.660 --> 00:09:15.220
It's all about fighting the urge to let fear dictate the response.

204
00:09:15.220 --> 00:09:20.700
Which brings us to a phenomenon where fear and panic are almost guaranteed human reactions.

205
00:09:20.700 --> 00:09:23.180
Non-suicidal self-injury or NSSI.

206
00:09:23.620 --> 00:09:24.620
Yeah.

207
00:09:24.620 --> 00:09:26.120
This is a tough one.

208
00:09:26.120 --> 00:09:31.460
We've just established that suicidal ideation is about the intent to end life.

209
00:09:31.460 --> 00:09:36.620
But what happens when you encounter severe self-harm, where the intent is actually the

210
00:09:36.620 --> 00:09:38.280
exact opposite?

211
00:09:38.280 --> 00:09:43.260
We are talking about individuals using harmful behaviors as a desperate attempt to cope with

212
00:09:43.260 --> 00:09:44.260
staying alive.

213
00:09:44.260 --> 00:09:45.260
Yes.

214
00:09:45.260 --> 00:09:49.780
The crucial distinction here is right in the name, non-suicidal.

215
00:09:49.780 --> 00:09:52.860
This is self-harm without the intent to die.

216
00:09:52.860 --> 00:09:54.340
So what is it about?

217
00:09:54.340 --> 00:09:59.700
It is almost always a mechanism for regulating emotion that has become completely unbearable.

218
00:09:59.700 --> 00:10:05.580
It provides this intense physical sensation that momentarily overrides or releases psychological

219
00:10:05.580 --> 00:10:06.580
agony.

220
00:10:06.580 --> 00:10:10.380
So the foundational rule is that you do not automatically treat it as a suicide attempt?

221
00:10:10.380 --> 00:10:11.380
Correct.

222
00:10:11.380 --> 00:10:13.060
I imagine human nature works heavily against us here.

223
00:10:13.060 --> 00:10:17.580
I mean, the site of self-harm triggers intense alarm bells for parents, friends, even novice

224
00:10:17.580 --> 00:10:18.580
professionals.

225
00:10:18.580 --> 00:10:19.580
Absolutely.

226
00:10:19.580 --> 00:10:20.580
It's terrifying to see.

227
00:10:20.580 --> 00:10:23.840
I have two incorrect reactions people usually have, and I realize they are actually just

228
00:10:23.840 --> 00:10:26.260
two sides of the same fearful coin.

229
00:10:26.260 --> 00:10:30.380
On one side, you panic and auto-hospitalize every single instance as if it were a suicide

230
00:10:30.380 --> 00:10:31.380
attempt.

231
00:10:31.380 --> 00:10:35.860
And on the other side, the behavior makes you so uncomfortable that you dismiss it entirely,

232
00:10:35.860 --> 00:10:38.340
just writing it off as attention-seeking.

233
00:10:38.340 --> 00:10:41.880
Both of those reactions completely miss the function of the behavior.

234
00:10:41.880 --> 00:10:42.880
How so?

235
00:10:42.880 --> 00:10:48.100
Well, if you hospitalize, you are over-restricting and punishing a coping mechanism without

236
00:10:48.100 --> 00:10:49.520
replacing it.

237
00:10:49.520 --> 00:10:54.320
If you dismiss it as attention-seeking, you are invalidating deep pain and missing the

238
00:10:54.320 --> 00:10:55.580
chance to intervene.

239
00:10:55.580 --> 00:10:57.480
So what is the correct approach?

240
00:10:57.480 --> 00:11:00.800
The correct approach is to conduct a functional assessment.

241
00:11:00.800 --> 00:11:07.440
You are systematically asking, what psychological need is this physical behavior meeting for

242
00:11:07.440 --> 00:11:08.640
this person right now?

243
00:11:08.640 --> 00:11:13.480
Okay, so once you assist the function, say they are doing it to numb a feeling of overwhelming

244
00:11:13.480 --> 00:11:16.280
panic, what is the actual intervention?

245
00:11:16.280 --> 00:11:20.860
The notes mention pairing that insight with DBT skills, dialectical behavior therapy, specifically

246
00:11:20.860 --> 00:11:22.720
something called distress tolerance.

247
00:11:22.720 --> 00:11:24.980
But what does that actually look like in practice?

248
00:11:24.980 --> 00:11:29.360
Distress tolerance is about giving the brain an intense sensory experience that resets the

249
00:11:29.360 --> 00:11:32.100
nervous system, but without causing tissue damage.

250
00:11:32.100 --> 00:11:36.860
So instead of self-harming to release emotional pressure, distress tolerance teaches a person

251
00:11:36.860 --> 00:11:42.880
to say, hold an ice cube tightly in their hand until it melts, or to plunge their

252
00:11:42.880 --> 00:11:45.800
face into a bowl of freezing cold water.

253
00:11:45.800 --> 00:11:46.800
Really?

254
00:11:46.800 --> 00:11:47.800
Freezing water?

255
00:11:47.800 --> 00:11:48.800
Yeah.

256
00:11:48.800 --> 00:11:53.920
